::ALG1_U03_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which points are in a table for y = x + 2 using x = 0, 1, 2? {
~A. (0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3) #This uses y = x + 1.
=B. (0, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4) #Correct. Add 2 to each x-value.
~C. (2, 0), (3, 1), (4, 2) #This reverses x- and y-values.
~D. (0, 2), (1, 2), (2, 2) #This shows a constant output, not y = x + 2.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of y = 4x + 6? {
=A. (0, 6) #Correct. The y-intercept occurs when x = 0.
~B. (6, 0) #This reverses the intercept coordinates.
~C. (4, 6) #This uses the coefficient as an x-value.
~D. (0, 4) #This uses the rate as the intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Is (3, 11) on the graph of y = 3x + 2? {
~A. No, because 3 + 11 is 14. #A point is checked by substituting x into the equation.
~B. No, because 3(3) + 2 = 9. #3(3) + 2 equals 11, not 9.
=C. Yes, because 3(3) + 2 = 11. #Correct. The equation gives the y-value 11.
~D. Yes, because 3(11) + 2 = 35. #Use the x-value 3, not the y-value 11.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is on y = -x + 5? {
~A. (0, -5) #When x = 0, y = 5.
~B. (1, 5) #When x = 1, y = 4.
~C. (5, 5) #When x = 5, y = 0.
=D. (2, 3) #Correct. -2 + 5 = 3.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has y-intercept (0, 8)? {
~A. y = 8x + 2 #This has y-intercept (0, 2).
~B. y = x - 8 #This has y-intercept (0, -8).
~C. y = 8x #This has y-intercept (0, 0).
=D. y = 2x + 8 #Correct. When x = 0, y = 8.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which table matches y = 2x - 1 for x = 0, 1, 2? {
~A. (0, 2), (1, 1), (2, 3) #This does not follow the equation.
=B. (0, -1), (1, 1), (2, 3) #Correct. Substitute each x-value into y = 2x - 1.
~C. (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 5) #This uses y = 2x + 1.
~D. (-1, 0), (1, 1), (3, 2) #This reverses or mismatches values.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does the point (0, 12) usually represent in a savings graph? {
=A. The starting amount before any weeks pass #Correct. At x = 0, the savings amount is 12.
~B. The amount saved after 12 weeks #The input is 0, not 12.
~C. The rate of change #The y-intercept is not the rate.
~D. The x-intercept #The point is on the y-axis, not the x-axis.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the x-intercept of the line through (0, 6), (2, 4), (4, 2), (6, 0)? {
~A. (0, 6) #This is the y-intercept.
~B. (2, 4) #This point is not on the x-axis.
=C. (6, 0) #Correct. The x-intercept has y = 0.
~D. (4, 2) #This point is not on the x-axis.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which first step helps graph y = 3x + 2 by table? {
~A. Set every y-value equal to 0 first. #That only helps find an x-intercept, not a full graph by table.
=B. Choose x-values to substitute into the equation. #Correct. Substitution creates ordered pairs for graphing.
~C. Reverse every ordered pair. #Reversing coordinates creates incorrect points.
~D. Plot only the number 3. #A graph needs ordered pairs, not one coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is the y-intercept of a graph? {
~A. A point where x = 5 #The y-intercept specifically has x = 0.
~B. A point where y = 0 #That describes an x-intercept.
=C. A point where x = 0 #Correct. The y-intercept is where the graph crosses the y-axis.
~D. A point with equal coordinates #Equal coordinates do not define an intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is the x-intercept of a graph? {
~A. A point where x = 0 #That describes a y-intercept.
~B. A point where y = x #That does not define an intercept.
~C. A point where y is positive #Many points can have positive y-values.
=D. A point where y = 0 #Correct. The x-intercept is where the graph crosses the x-axis.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph description matches y = -2x + 4? {
~A. It crosses the y-axis at (0, -2). #The y-intercept is (0, 4).
=B. It crosses the y-axis at (0, 4) and decreases by 2 for each x-step. #Correct. The intercept is 4 and the rate is -2.
~C. It rises 4 for each step in x. #The rate is -2, so the graph decreases.
~D. It has no y-intercept. #Every equation in this form has a y-intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is on y = 5x? {
=A. (2, 10) #Correct. 5(2) = 10.
~B. (5, 2) #This reverses the matching input and output.
~C. (1, 6) #When x = 1, y = 5.
~D. (0, 5) #When x = 0, y = 0.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>For y = x - 3, which ordered pair is correct when x = 4? {
~A. (4, 7) #This adds 3 instead of subtracting.
~B. (1, 4) #This reverses the coordinates.
~C. (-3, 4) #This uses the constant as an x-value.
=D. (4, 1) #Correct. 4 - 3 = 1.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>A graph crosses the y-axis at (0, 9). What is true? {
~A. The x-intercept is 9. #A y-axis crossing is a y-intercept, not an x-intercept.
~B. The rate of change is 9. #The intercept alone does not give the rate.
=C. The output is 9 when the input is 0. #Correct. The point (0, 9) means x = 0 and y = 9.
~D. The graph cannot be linear. #Linear graphs can cross the y-axis at (0, 9).
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which ordered pair should be plotted from x = 2 and y = -1? {
~A. (-1, 2) #This reverses x and y.
=B. (2, -1) #Correct. Ordered pairs are written (x, y).
~C. (2, 1) #The y-value should be -1.
~D. (-2, 1) #Both signs or positions are incorrect.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement explains why (1, 4) is on y = 2x + 2? {
=A. Substituting x = 1 gives y = 4. #Correct. 2(1) + 2 = 4.
~B. Substituting y = 1 gives x = 4. #To check a point, substitute the x-value into the equation.
~C. The coordinates add to 5. #Adding coordinates does not check the equation.
~D. The point is on every line. #A point belongs only to graphs whose equations it satisfies.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which table matches y = -x + 3 for x = 0, 1, 2? {
~A. (0, -3), (1, -2), (2, -1) #This uses y = x - 3.
~B. (0, 3), (1, 4), (2, 5) #This uses y = x + 3.
=C. (0, 3), (1, 2), (2, 1) #Correct. Substitute each x-value into y = -x + 3.
~D. (3, 0), (2, 1), (1, 2) #The input and output values are reversed.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is not on y = x + 4? {
~A. (0, 4) #This point is on the line.
~B. (1, 5) #This point is on the line.
~C. (3, 7) #This point is on the line.
=D. (2, 5) #Correct. When x = 2, y should be 6.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>If a line passes through (0, 0), what is its y-intercept? {
=A. (0, 0) #Correct. The graph crosses the y-axis at the origin.
~B. (1, 0) #This has x = 1, so it is not on the y-axis.
~C. (0, 1) #The given point has y = 0.
~D. No y-intercept #The point (0, 0) is on the y-axis.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is true about graphing a linear equation from a table? {
~A. Only the y-values are plotted. #Graphing requires both coordinates.
=B. Each row of the table gives an ordered pair to plot. #Correct. The x- and y-values become graph points.
~C. The x-values are ignored. #The x-values determine horizontal position.
~D. The table must have exactly one row. #Multiple rows help show the line.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y = 4x - 1? {
~A. (0, 1) #When x = 0, y = -1.
~B. (2, 6) #When x = 2, y = 7.
=C. (1, 3) #Correct. 4(1) - 1 = 3.
~D. (3, 10) #When x = 3, y = 11.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of y = -3x - 2? {
=A. (0, -2) #Correct. When x = 0, y = -2.
~B. (0, 3) #The sign and value are incorrect.
~C. (-3, 0) #This uses the coefficient as an x-coordinate.
~D. (-2, 0) #This reverses the intercept coordinates.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>A line contains (0, 5), (1, 7), and (2, 9). What does the graph do as x increases by 1? {
~A. y decreases by 2. #The y-values increase, not decrease.
~B. y increases by 1. #The x-values increase by 1; y increases by 2.
~C. y stays the same. #The y-values change.
=D. y increases by 2. #Correct. The output changes from 5 to 7 to 9.
}

::ALG1_U03_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Why is (0, b) important on many linear graphs? {
=A. It shows the output when the input is 0. #Correct. This is the y-intercept or starting value.
~B. It always shows the final output. #Linear graphs may continue beyond shown points.
~C. It shows the x-intercept. #The x-intercept has y = 0.
~D. It means the graph is not linear. #A linear graph can have a y-intercept.
}
